interface: count a change of the adaptive flag as a runtime change

0027 starts adaptive input nodes polling. When the last polling queue of a
thread turns adaptive, the wanted node state therefore stays polling, and
the update only goes ahead if the polling vector, the interrupt count or
rxq_vector_poll differ. rxq_vector_poll is only replaced while the node is
adaptive, so after adaptive -> polling -> adaptive it still holds the same
queues; the interrupt count changes only if the queue turned last has the
highest queue index. Otherwise the update is skipped, the node never gets
VLIB_NODE_FLAG_ADAPTIVE_MODE and keeps polling at 100% CPU with an empty
ring. On the RK3588 board, wan0 and lan0 set polling and then lan0, wan0
adaptive left dpdk-input polling; on the ConnectX-3 host the same happened
with port0 turned adaptive last. Boot sets NICs in creation order, so the
last one has the highest index and boot was not affected. Compare the
node's adaptive flag with the wanted one.

--- a/src/vnet/interface/runtime.c
+++ b/src/vnet/interface/runtime.c
@@ -162,6 +162,19 @@
 		     node_state_str[per_thread_node_state[i]]);
 	}

+      /* An adaptive node starts polling, so turning the last polling queue
+	 of a thread adaptive leaves the state unchanged, and the vectors
+	 below may still match the ones from an earlier adaptive setup: the
+	 node would keep polling without the adaptive flag. Compare it too. */
+      if (!(vlib_node_get_runtime (ovm, node_index)->flags &
+	    VLIB_NODE_FLAG_ADAPTIVE_MODE) != !per_thread_node_adaptive[i])
+	{
+	  something_changed_on_rx = 1;
+	  log_debug ("adaptive mode changed for node %U on thread %u to %u",
+		     format_vlib_node_name, vm, node_index, i,
+		     per_thread_node_adaptive[i]);
+	}
+
       /* check if something changed */
       if (something_changed_on_rx == 0)
 	{
